DEEP[1]=('Chapter 14 is named Guṇa-Traya-Vibhāga Yoga, the yoga of the division of the three guṇas. It is the Gītā’s fullest account of the three qualities of nature, which shape every mind, body and action.',
 'The guṇas come from Sāṅkhya philosophy, but the Gītā uses them practically: as a map of the mind. Śaṅkara says the teaching is repeated to create dispassion toward the guṇas. Rāmānuja says it shows how the self is bound to matter and how it can be freed. The theory of guṇas also shaped Āyurveda.',
 'Understanding how your moods and habits work is already the beginning of freedom from them.')
DEEP[2]=('',
 '<i>Sādharmya</i>, “having the same dharma” or nature as the Lord, is a key word in the Vedānta debates. Śaṅkara takes it as oneness. Rāmānuja takes it as likeness: the freed soul shares the Lord’s purity and bliss but remains a distinct self. Madhva insists on distinction even in freedom. The Brahma Sūtras (4.4.17–21) discuss what powers the freed soul has; all agree it is free of the cycle.',
 'Lasting peace does not depend on what happens outside. It rests on what you know inside.')
DEEP[3]=('',
 'The image is of cosmic parenthood. <i>Mahad brahma</i>, “great Brahman”, here means <i>prakṛti</i>, primal matter, as Śaṅkara and Rāmānuja both explain. The “seed” is consciousness, the higher nature of 7.5. Sāṅkhya taught that matter evolves when spirit is near; the Gītā adds that the Lord brings them together. Many Hindu images show the Divine as father and mother together, as in Ardhanārīśvara, Śiva and Pārvatī in one body.',
 'Every living being carries a divine seed. Treat all life with reverence.')
DEEP[4]=('',
 'Indian tradition speaks of 8.4 million species, <i>caurāsī lakh yoni</i>, through which souls pass. Commentators say the verse covers all of them: plants, animals, humans and gods. Śaṅkara says all bodies come from nature and the Lord as consciousness. Rāmānuja says the Lord unites each soul with its body. Many find in this verse a basis for kindness to all creatures and care for the earth.',
 'All creatures are family, with one father. Let that shape how you treat animals and nature.')
DEEP[5]=('',
 '<i>Guṇa</i> also means a strand of rope; the three strands twist together to bind. The Sāṅkhya Kārikā (12–13) describes sattva as light and pleasant, rajas as moving and stimulating, tamas as heavy and covering. They work together like the wick, oil and flame of a lamp. Commentators note that the Self is called <i>avyaya</i>, imperishable, even while bound: the binding is not of its nature.',
 'Notice which strand is strongest in you right now: clarity, restlessness or heaviness.')
DEEP[6]=('',
 'Ramakrishna told a famous story. Three robbers stood for the three guṇas. Tamas wanted to kill a traveller, rajas to tie him up, and sattva freed him and led him to the edge of the forest. But sattva would not go home with him, for fear of the police. Sattva brings you near freedom but cannot take you all the way. Commentators say the pride of the learned or the good is the golden chain.',
 'Notice attachment even to good things: being right, being calm, knowing more. Hold them gently.')
DEEP[7]=('',
 '<i>Tṛṣṇā</i>, thirst or craving, is also the Buddha’s word (<i>taṇhā</i>) for the cause of suffering. Commentators note that rajas is necessary for action: without it nothing gets done. The problem is its craving. Śaṅkara says rajas binds by making one think “I must do this to get that”. Āyurveda links rajas with spicy, stimulating foods and fast living.',
 'Before starting the next thing, ask: am I acting from purpose or from restlessness?')
DEEP[8]=('',
 'Tamas is necessary too: sleep restores, stability holds things together. The problem is excess. Commentators list three marks: <i>pramāda</i>, carelessness, not attending to what matters; <i>ālasya</i>, sloth, not wanting to act; <i>nidrā</i>, too much sleep or dullness. Śaṅkara says tamas covers discrimination. Teachers advise moving from tamas to rajas first: get up and act, then aim at sattva.',
 'When you feel heavy and dull, take one small action. Movement is the first cure for fog.')
DEEP[9]=('',
 'A summary in one verse. Commentators often teach the ladder: from tamas to rajas, from rajas to sattva, then beyond. Some compare the guṇas to traffic lights: red (tamas) stops, yellow (rajas) rushes, green (sattva) flows. The aim is to drive with awareness, not to stop forever on green. Madhusūdana says even sattva must be offered to the Lord.',
 'Know which guṇa holds you today. Then you know what step to take next.')
DEEP[10]=('',
 'The guṇas are never found alone; they compete, and one dominates at a time. Āyurveda and yoga traditions link this to daily rhythms: early morning, <i>brāhma muhūrta</i>, is sāttvika and best for meditation. Commentators note that food (17.8–10), company and habits all tilt the balance. Śaṅkara says knowing this helps one guard against the lower guṇas.',
 'Small choices about food, sleep and company tip the balance. Choose with care.')
DEEP[11]=('',
 'Commentators describe the signs: the eyes see clearly, the ears listen well, the mind is calm and alert. Śaṅkara says the light of knowledge shines through the senses when sattva rules. In meditation traditions, this clarity is the ground for deeper practice. Many people notice it after rest, in nature, or after good company.',
 'Notice moments of clarity: in the early morning, after a walk, in good company. That is sattva shining.')
DEEP[12]=('',
 'Commentators list the marks of rajas: <i>lobha</i>, greed; <i>pravṛtti</i>, constant activity; <i>ārambha</i>, starting new projects; <i>aśama</i>, inability to rest; <i>spṛhā</i>, longing. Many modern readers recognise their own lives in this list. Śaṅkara notes that rajas is not always bad; it drives effort. But when it rules, peace vanishes.',
 'When you cannot stop starting things, rajas is high. Pause and breathe before the next.')
DEEP[13]=('',
 'Tamas is the hardest to see from inside, because it dims the very faculty of seeing. Commentators describe <i>aprakāśa</i>, lack of clarity; <i>apravṛtti</i>, not wanting to do anything; <i>pramāda</i>, carelessness; <i>moha</i>, confusion. Śaṅkara says tamas makes one mistake one thing for another. Teachers suggest simple remedies: fresh air, movement, light food, and the company of energetic people.',
 'If everything feels heavy, move a little: walk, clean, open a window. Start small.')
DEEP[14]=('',
 'This connects with 8.6: the state at death shapes the next life. Commentators note that dying in sattva leads to the pure worlds of the wise, from which one may continue toward freedom. Śaṅkara says the knowers of the highest are those who know the great principles. Rāmānuja says dying in sattva leads to birth among those who know the self. The advice: cultivate sattva in life so that it is present at the end.',
 'Live in clarity and kindness. What fills your days will fill your last moments.')
DEEP[15]=('',
 'Commentators read “those attached to action” as a human birth among busy, striving people. “Deluded wombs” are read as lower forms of life or confused human conditions. Śaṅkara and Rāmānuja take the verse literally. Many modern teachers read it psychologically: what we repeatedly do, we become. Either way, the verse is moral cause and effect, not a threat.',
 'Today’s habits shape tomorrow’s life. Choose them as if they matter, because they do.')
DEEP[16]=('',
 'Commentators note a subtle point: rajas looks productive but ends in pain. Ambition brings exhaustion, competition, fear of loss and craving for more. Śaṅkara says rajas yields pain because its actions are driven by desire. Chapter 18 (18.37–39) applies the same three-fold division to happiness: sāttvika happiness is like poison at first and nectar at the end; rājasika is nectar first and poison later.',
 'Judge actions by their fruit over time, not by how exciting they feel at the start.')
DEEP[17]=('',
 'This verse gives a simple diagnostic: watch what grows in the mind. If understanding grows, sattva is active; if wanting grows, rajas; if confusion grows, tamas. Commentators say this self-observation is itself a sāttvika act. Śaṅkara notes that knowing the effects helps one cultivate the right cause. Many meditation teachers use this verse to help students observe their states.',
 'Ask at the end of the day: did knowledge, greed or confusion grow in me today?')
DEEP[18]=('',
 'A simple map of direction. Commentators read “up” as higher worlds or higher consciousness, “middle” as the human world of striving, and “down” as lower births or darker states. Śaṅkara takes it as the destinies after death. Many teachers apply it to daily life: each choice lifts, holds or lowers. The idea of three directions appears across Indian thought, in the three worlds and three paths.',
 'Before a choice, ask: is this taking me up, keeping me level, or pulling me down?')
DEEP[19]=('',
 'Commentators link this to 3.27–28, 5.8–9 and 13.29. <i>Draṣṭṛ</i>, the seer, is the witness consciousness. Śaṅkara says when one sees the guṇas as the only actors and oneself as the witness, freedom comes. Rāmānuja says one sees the self as distinct from the guṇas and reaches the Lord. The image of a spectator at a play is common: the actors are the guṇas; the audience is the Self.',
 'Watch your moods as weather passing across the sky. You are the sky, not the clouds.')
DEEP[20]=('',
 '<i>Guṇātīta</i>, one beyond the guṇas, names the ideal of the chapter. Commentators stress that this does not mean destroying the guṇas; the body, made of them, continues. It means no longer being ruled by them. Śaṅkara says such a person is liberated while living. Rāmānuja says the self, freed from matter’s grip, enjoys its own bliss. The list of sorrows, birth, death, old age, recalls 13.8.',
 'Freedom is not changing your whole nature. It is no longer being ruled by it.')
DEEP[21]=('',
 'Arjuna asks three questions: the marks, the conduct, and the method. Commentators note that this mirrors 2.54 (the sthita-prajña) and anticipates nothing new but asks for practical clarity. Kṛṣṇa answers the first two in 14.22–25 and the third in 14.26. Śaṅkara says the marks of the one beyond the guṇas are also the means for the seeker.',
 'Ask how ideas look in real life. That is how understanding becomes practice.')
DEEP[22]=('',
 '<i>Prakāśa</i> is the mark of sattva, <i>pravṛtti</i> of rajas, <i>moha</i> of tamas. Commentators note the remarkable point: the free person does not even long for clarity or hate confusion. Śaṅkara says he sees them as modifications of the mind, not of the Self. Rāmānuja says he is not disturbed by any state of the body or mind. This is equanimity toward one’s own inner weather.',
 'Don’t fight your moods or chase them. Let them come and go, and stay steady underneath.')
DEEP[23]=('',
 '<i>Udāsīna</i> was used of the Lord in 9.9. Commentators note that the free person shares the Lord’s serene detachment. <i>Neṅgate</i>, “does not waver”, recalls the lamp in a windless place (6.19). Śaṅkara says the knower remains firm in the Self. The phrase <i>guṇā vartante</i> recalls 3.28. The image is of a mountain unmoved by passing winds.',
 'Practise watching rather than reacting. A breath of distance can change a whole day.')
DEEP[24]=('',
 '<i>Svastha</i>, “established in oneself”, is the everyday word for healthy in Hindi and many Indian languages. Āyurveda defines health, <i>svāsthya</i>, as balance of body and a contented mind and senses (Suśruta Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 15). Commentators enjoy the link: the one beyond the guṇas is truly healthy. Clay, stone and gold recall 6.8.',
 'To be truly healthy is to be at home in yourself. Rest there a little each day.')
DEEP[25]=('',
 'Commentators note that three portraits in the Gītā describe the same ideal person from different angles: the <i>sthita-prajña</i>, of steady wisdom (2.55–72); the <i>bhakta</i>, devotee (12.13–20); and the <i>guṇātīta</i>, beyond the guṇas (14.22–25). The marks overlap: evenness, freedom from hatred, contentment, self-control. Śaṅkara says they are one person seen from knowledge, devotion and freedom from nature.',
 'Different paths describe the same freedom. That is encouraging: you can approach it from your own side.')
DEEP[26]=('',
 'This verse answers Arjuna’s “how?”. Commentators of every school note its importance: devotion is the means to go beyond the guṇas. Śaṅkara says devotion here is unwavering knowledge-focused service of the Lord. Rāmānuja says it is loving meditation that frees from matter. <i>Brahma-bhūya</i>, becoming Brahman, recalls 5.24 and 18.53–54, where becoming Brahman leads to supreme devotion.',
 'Love is the simplest way out of the tangle of moods. Turn toward what you love most.')
DEEP[27]=('',
 '<i>Pratiṣṭhā</i> means foundation, abode or ground. Śaṅkara reads it as “I am the very substance of Brahman”. Rāmānuja reads it as “I am the support of the freed self’s state”. Gauḍīya Vaiṣṇavas read it as Kṛṣṇa the person being the ground even of the impersonal Brahman, as the sun is the source of its rays. We note these readings without choosing among them. The colophon names chapter 14 Guṇa-Traya-Vibhāga Yoga.',
 'Behind every ideal, truth, dharma and joy, there is one source. Seek it.')
